NEW IN THE SECOND EDITION
=========================

A running log of what changed between the 1st edition (frozen in
paperback_edition_1/) and the 2nd edition (the repo root). Kept so the preface can
be rewritten from it at the end of the work — so entries are phrased as "what a
reader of the 1st edition would find different", not as commit messages.

Baseline: at the 1st-edition commit the root .tex files were byte-identical to
paperback_edition_1/, so `diff` against that directory always shows the full delta.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-07-15 — Chapter 7, "Boosting shortest path multiplicity": rewritten
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Brought up to date with the newest version of the underlying paper, "Local network
evolution rules drive shortest path multiplicity" (arXiv:2605.25237), whose source
is at github.com/av2atgh/ramsey_netcom/tree/master/local_multi_path_2026. The 1st
edition was written against an earlier version that lacked the mean-field theory,
the diamond lattice, and the real-network data.

NEW SCIENCE (things the 1st edition simply did not contain)

  * A mean-field theory of the multiplicity. The chapter now derives, rather than
    asserts, the random-network baseline: <mu> ~ c_2/e, where c_2 = <k(k-1)>/<k>
    is the average excess degree — the branching factor of the network. This is
    the single most important addition: the 1st edition had no calculation here at
    all, and the new box means the baseline is understood rather than measured.

  * The three regimes. Because c_2 is controlled by the hubs, the baseline depends
    entirely on the degree exponent: <mu> is constant for gamma > 3, logarithmic at
    gamma = 3, and a power law n^(3/gamma - 1) for 2 < gamma < 3. The 1st edition
    had a single, universal logarithmic baseline (which is wrong — see below).

  * The diamond lattice — an exactly solvable ceiling. A new section asks how fast
    multiplicity could possibly grow, and answers it exactly. The pole-to-pole
    count is doubly exponential in the generation, P_t = 2^(2^t - 1), which in terms
    of network size is a stretched exponential, <mu> ~ a exp(b sqrt(n)) with
    b = sqrt(3/2) ln 2 ~ 0.849. By the sixth generation a 2732-node lattice already
    joins a typical pair by ~7e15 distinct shortest paths. Perfect, maximally
    symmetric branching therefore bounds the redundancy any growing network can
    carry, and every stochastic local rule falls below it.

  * Real networks. A new section, and the chapter's first contact with data: the
    Internet at the autonomous-system level, protein interaction networks of a
    dozen organisms, and cond-mat co-authorship. The human protein network reaches
    <mu> ~ 64 — a typical pair of proteins joined by sixty-four equally short chains
    of interaction — and the protein networks are the ones that climb exponentially,
    the duplication signature.

  * Degree correlations modulate the redundancy. The real data show which side of
    the mean-field line a system falls on, and it tracks assortativity: the
    disassortative Internet sits below its random expectation (a star gives a route
    no alternatives), the assortative co-authorship network above it (a clique of
    hubs furnishes many equally short ways across). The mean field, which knows only
    c_2, cannot yet account for this. Named in the chapter as an open invitation.

CORRECTIONS (places where the 1st edition is wrong, not merely thin)

  * The universal logarithmic baseline is false. "Sparse random networks accumulate
    multiplicity only logarithmically" holds only at gamma = 3. Corrected to the
    three regimes above.

  * The duplication-split degree exponent was given as gamma = 1 + 1/q. It is
    gamma = 1/q. Chapter 5 (Activity specialization) already said 1/q, so the 1st
    edition contradicted itself across chapters. The cross-reference was also
    retargeted from Chapter 4 to Chapter 5, duplication-split being Chapter 5's model.

  * The randomized local-search network does not creep up logarithmically; it
    saturates, having gamma = 5 > 3. The figure now shows the points peeling away
    from the fitted logarithm.

  * The point about the deterministic pseudofractal had the wrong sign. The 1st
    edition said it "grows only log-linearly, like the shuffled baseline". In fact
    its randomized version follows a power law while the deterministic original stays
    log-linear — it falls BELOW its own shuffle. The moral is stronger than before:
    clockwork locality does worse than chance, so randomness and locality are needed
    together.

  * "This closing chapter" — it is not the closing chapter; the Outlook is.

ALL THE PAPER'S CALCULATIONS NOW CARRIED IN FULL (second pass, same day)

  The first pass summarized the appendix; the chapter now derives it. 488 -> 784
  lines, three calculation boxes -> eight, pages 79-100.

  * The Erdos-Renyi count in full: candidate paths m_d ~ n^(d-1), realized
    <M_d> = m_d p^d = c^(d-D), the no-shorter-path weight F_d = exp(-c^(d-D)) from
    Pr(M_d=0) = (1-p^d)^m_d, the sum <mu> = sum_d <M_d> F_(d-1), and the sharp peak
    one hop beyond the typical distance giving <mu> ~ c/e. The three ER regimes
    (sparse constant, minimally connected (b/e) ln n, dense ~ n, the last recovering
    dong2025) now follow from it rather than being asserted.
  * The arbitrary-degree-distribution repair, which is the conceptual heart: after
    the first step a path always stands at the END of a link, where the degree is
    distributed as k pi_k / c_1 and one link is already spent — so the branching is
    the EXCESS degree. p_1 = c_1/n but p_2 = c_2/n, and c_1 is replaced by c_2
    everywhere it matters, giving <mu> ~ c_2/e and recovering ER when c_2 = c_1.
  * How c_2 grows: k_max from p(k_max) = 1/n giving k_max ~ n^(1/gamma), then
    c_2 ~ ln n at gamma=3 and c_2 ~ n^(-1+3/gamma) below it. The gamma=3 case is
    done explicitly — p_k = A/[(2+k)(1+k)k], <k> ~ A/3 by a telescoping sum that
    converges, <k^2> ~ (A/3) ln n which does not.
  * NEW SECTION 7.4, "Which rule makes which exponent". The rate equation
    n_k(n+1) = n_k(n) + [a+b(k-1)]/n n_{k-1} - (a+bk)/n n_k + c delta_k2 + d/n n_k,
    the steady state n_k = n p_k, the recursion in k alone with eta = a/b and
    nu = (1+a-d)/b, its closed form in Gamma functions, and the asymptotic
    gamma = 1 + (1-d)/b. One master key: only the preferential rate b and the
    duplication rate d survive. Read off each rule's constants and its exponent
    falls out — gamma_BA = 3, gamma_LS = (3m+2)/m, gamma_DS = 1/q, gamma_BB = L+2.
    (All four verified against the master formula before being written down.)
    Two coincidences the section makes load-bearing: DS(n,q) and BB(n,L) share a
    degree distribution when q = (L+2)^-1, so any difference in their multiplicity
    cannot be blamed on degrees; and DS(1/3), BB(1), BA(2) all sit at gamma=3 —
    three rules, one degree distribution, three different multiplicities. This is
    what makes the chapter's comparisons fair, and the 1st edition never said it.
  * The diamond in its general (b,s) form: the deflation argument (series multiply,
    parallel add), D_t = s^t and P_t = b P_{t-1}^s, linearized by log_b into
    P_t = b^((s^t-1)/(s-1)), checked against direct construction for five (b,s)
    pairs. Then the two steps the first pass skipped: every vertex lies on a
    pole-to-pole geodesic (d(v,A)+d(v,B) = D_t, by induction), which makes the
    lattice effectively one-dimensional and gives S_t = 4 S_{t-1} + Inter_t, whence
    <mu>_t ~ kappa 2^(2^t)/4^t with kappa -> 1.576; and the inversion of the node
    count, 2^t ~ sqrt(3n/2), turning the doubly exponential in t into the stretched
    exponential in n with b = sqrt(3/2) ln2 exact and a = 2 kappa/3 ~ 1.05.
  * The four models are now defined exactly (initial conditions included — DS starts
    from a 4-cycle, BB from an (L+2)-cycle), because the chapter computes their
    exponents and the constants depend on it.

FIGURES

  The chapter went from one hand-drawn schematic and no data whatsoever to five
  figures: the square-vs-triangle schematic (kept), the random baseline, the boost
  from local rules, cycle length and randomness, the diamond lattice, and the real
  networks. This is the first data to appear anywhere in the book.

  A reproducible pipeline now lives in figs/: mkfigs.py plus the simulation and
  real-network CSVs copied from the paper's repository, and multiplicity_diamond.py,
  the exact-arithmetic checker that reproduces the diamond results from scratch. The
  figures are deliberately not reprints of the paper's — grayscale for the black-and-
  white printing, panels regrouped for the 5x8 trim, Computer Modern maths.

STILL OPEN

  * The exponential law for duplication-split is motivated (square-counting plus
    compounding) but not derived. Open upstream too, and open problem #1 in the
    Outlook.
  * The modulation of the multiplicity by degree correlations is observed, not
    calculated.

PREFACE IMPLICATIONS (for the rewrite at the end)

  * The 1st-edition preface says the closing chapters "push that idea furthest". That
    is now more true and differently true: chapter 7 has a derivation, an exactly
    solvable limit, and data.
  * Chapters 6 and 7 are now matched in weight (~1120 and ~780 lines, nine and eight
    calculation boxes) and share a lattice, a scale (sqrt(n)) and a theme (cycles).
    Together they are the technical core of the book, and the preface's promise that
    the technical parts sit in skippable boxes is strained by both. Consider saying
    outright that the last two chapters reward a reader willing to open the boxes.
  * The preface's promise that "the empirical data set the agenda throughout" was not
    honoured by the 1st edition, which contained no data plots at all. Chapter 7 now
    honours it. If the other chapters gain data figures, the claim becomes accurate
    for the book as a whole; if they do not, the sentence should be softened.
  * Acknowledgement of Claude's help with prose and figures carries over and, on the
    figure side, now covers more.
  * Consider naming the diamond lattice in the preface's per-chapter tour: it is the
    one exactly solvable result in the book.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-07-15 — Chapter 6, "Emergence of communities": three exactly solvable cases
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The 1st-edition chapter rested entirely on simulation: every Ramsey community
number in it (81, 98, 108, 1090, ...) was a numerical estimate, and the chapter
could say THAT communities appear beyond a certain size but never WHY, nor what
the size depends on. Three new papers — the "wants to be broken" series — make
r_kappa exactly solvable, and the chapter now ends on theory rather than numerics.
Sections 6.1-6.5 are unchanged; six new sections (6.6-6.11) and a rewritten summary
follow, with all the papers' calculations carried in full rather than summarized.
The chapter roughly triples in length (346 -> ~1120 lines, pages 49-76).

NEW: THE RING (arXiv:2607.01967)

  A featureless ring of beads — no growth, no randomness, no heterogeneity, every
  node identical — is nonetheless split into two arcs by a block model, once each
  bead reaches past its nearest neighbour. The plain cycle (c=1) is never split;
  the next-nearest-neighbour ring (c=2) breaks at r_kappa ~ 34. This is the
  cleanest possible statement of the book's thesis: locality alone suffices, with
  every other ingredient stripped away.

  Framed as SPONTANEOUS SYMMETRY BREAKING, which is both honest and the better
  physics. The ring is vertex-transitive, so all n rotations of the balanced cut
  have identical evidence: the block model says the ring must be cut SOMEWHERE
  while staying silent about where. Like a magnet below T_c choosing a direction,
  the symmetry survives in the multiplicity of outcomes, not in any one of them.
  The communities are real (the split describes the network better) but degenerate
  (no particular split is preferred). The paper does not discuss the degeneracy;
  the chapter does.

NEW RESULT, NOT IN ANY OF THE PAPERS (flagged as such here)

  The chapter derives, and Fig. 6.4b verifies for c=1..4:

      log R  =  (m_edges - n) ln 2  =  (c-1) n ln 2

  from a competition between a gain of ln 2 per edge (each arc looks twice as
  dense) and a cost of log C(n, n/2) ~ n ln 2 for saying which nodes go where.
  The label-prior term is EXACTLY -log C(n,n/2) at alpha=1 (verified to 78 decimal
  places, and provable in two lines from B(m+1,m+1)/B(1,n+1) = 1/C(n,n/2)).

  Consequences worth having:
  * It dissolves the paper's headline c=1 vs c=2 contrast. The cycle is not
    "reluctant" to break, it is EXACTLY MARGINAL: m = n, so the O(n) terms cancel
    identically and the subleading -(2alpha+3)log n decides. The celebrated sharp
    threshold is a knife-edge cancellation, not a transition; c=1 sits at the zero
    of a smooth family and the evidence grows linearly in c-1 thereafter.
  * It CORRECTS the paper's stated explanation. The manuscript says "a plain cycle
    offers no density contrast to exploit". That is false: the within-arc/whole-ring
    density contrast is ~2 for EVERY c including the cycle (verified: 1.998, 1.996,
    1.994 for c=1,2,3 at n=1024). What differs is how many edges enjoy the contrast,
    not the contrast. The chapter does not repeat the paper's reason.
  * Since m-n+1 is the number of independent cycles, communities are preferred
    exactly when a network carries an extensive number of independent cycles. A
    tree never splits; a single ring sits exactly on the fence. This ties chapter 6
    to chapter 7 — the same short loops that multiply shortest paths carve out
    communities — and to the diamond's "ln K per edge" below. It is the strongest
    unification the book has.

NEW: THE DIAMOND LATTICE (arXiv:2607.05954)

  Supplies the mechanism the chapter never had. Because the bundles meet only at
  the poles, the block-model tallies renormalize under an exact linear map with
  eigenvalues {lambda_vol, lambda_seam} = {bs, b}; their ratio 1/s is the whole
  story. For s >= 2 the seam is left behind by the bulk and the split improves
  without limit; for s = 1 the two rates match and the lattice never separates —
  the diamond's exact counterpart of the plain cycle, which is a pleasing echo of
  the ring. b sets how many communities; s sets how sharply they separate.

  The mechanism, in one line: the densities flow to a fixed point (u_in, u_cross)
  -> (K, 0), each edge contributes ln K to the evidence, so log R / m -> ln K —
  lattice-independent, and the same ln 2 per edge that broke the ring. The evidence
  is EXTENSIVE while the price of positing a partition grows only LOGARITHMICALLY,
  so a quantity growing like m ln K must overtake one growing like ln m. Emergence
  is not delicate; it is arithmetic. r_kappa merely records where the crossing
  falls, and for the whole (b,s) family it is now a closed form rather than a
  simulation. Same lattice as chapter 7, so the setup costs nothing.

  The general (b,s) family is carried in full: the bundle lemma, the triangular
  recursion c_{t+1} = b c_t, i_{t+1} = bs i_t + b(s-1) c_t, its closed solution,
  the density ratios, the seam deficit delta_t, and the closed form

      r_kappa(b,s;q) = 2 + [b(s-1)/(bs-1)] [(bs)^{t*} - 1],
      t* = ceil( log_bs( ln[q/(1-q)] / ln b ) ).

  The degrees, the priors and the lattice details all drop out — only b (entropy
  per edge) and s (volume growth) survive. A quantity that three sections earlier
  needed thousands of simulated networks is now a formula.

NEW: THE PSEUDOFRACTAL WEB (arXiv:2607.03010)

  Answers the sharpest doubt: is what we call communities just the hubs in
  disguise? A block model handed a scale-free network can improve its fit merely by
  absorbing the degree distribution. Correct for the degree sequence — score
  against a null that already reproduces every degree — and the answer reverses:
  the plain model prefers the hub-leaf cut (chasing the degree gradient, exactly as
  feared), while the corrected model demotes it and elevates the recursive branch
  cut. The communities are real; they survive a null that knows every degree.

  And the verdicts on WHEN disagree twenty-five-fold on the same web, same
  partition: r_kappa = 1095 plain, 42 degree-corrected. Counterintuitively the
  degree gradient DELAYS the plain detector rather than driving it.

CORRECTION TO THE 1st EDITION'S FRAMING

  The 1st edition treats the choice of detection method as cosmetic: "the exact
  figures differ between the two detection methods ... but the split between local
  rules (finite) and non-local ones (infinite) is the same either way." The 25x
  plain/corrected gap shows the dependence is not cosmetic. A caution is now lodged
  in section 6.2 when r_kappa is defined, and collected in 6.9: r_kappa was never a
  property of the network alone but of the network together with the question asked
  of it. The thesis survives — under either rule the local construction has a
  finite r_kappa and a non-local one has none — but a bare Ramsey number quoted
  without its detection rule says less than it appears to. This is also progress on
  open problem #2 in the Outlook ("make r_kappa algorithm-independent"): these
  papers quantify the dependence rather than removing it, and the fixed-point
  density ln K is the lattice-independent piece that does survive.

HONEST CAVEATS CARRIED IN THE CHAPTER

  * All three solvable networks are deterministic lattices, not grown networks.
    What they isolate is locality stripped of every other ingredient — stated
    plainly in 6.6 rather than glossed.
  * No detection algorithm is run in any of them; the partition is supplied and its
    evidence evaluated. They answer "when does the known split become the better
    description?", not "can an algorithm find it?"

TWO FURTHER SECTIONS (6.10, 6.11)

  * "How many communities?" — dropping the two-block convenience. The web's
    symmetric three-way cut has density ln 3 per edge exactly as the fixed point
    demands, beating the branch cut by (4/3) ln2 n — the evidence forfeited by
    lumping two communities together. Refinement keeps paying (+2 ln3 n per level)
    until the seams bite, and the balance gives q_opt ~ sqrt(gamma n): the number of
    communities that best describes a network GROWS WITH IT, about sqrt(n)
    communities of sqrt(n) nodes, the geometric midpoint of its own hierarchy. Same
    sqrt(n) as chapter 7's path multiplicity on the same lattice — both are the seam
    scale b^t in disguise.

  * "Communities as a state of matter" — the Reichardt-Bornholdt Hamiltonian, whose
    ground state is the partition and which at gamma=1 is modularity itself. For two
    communities it is an Ising ferromagnet plus an infinite-range degree-weighted
    penalty, solvable exactly on the diamond by decimation: below k_B T_c/J = 1.641
    the two poles lock into opposite communities for ANY resolution gamma>0, and the
    order survives as n -> infinity. (A plain ferromagnet on the same lattice has no
    such phase — its ground state is the trivial single community. It is the penalty
    that changes the ground state into a partition.) With q = 2^d colours the Potts
    decimation gives a cascade of first-order transitions at k_B T_c(q)/J ~ 1/(c ln q),
    c ~ 0.70, so the hierarchy orders level by level, coarsest first, and every stable
    level persists as n grows. Communities are not merely inferable; they are ordered
    — a state the network is in.

FIGURES

  Three new: fig-com-ring (the c=1/c=2 contrast, and the new (c-1)n ln2 law),
  fig-com-rg (the evidence crossing that IS r_kappa, and the flow to the fixed
  point), fig-com-degree (plain vs degree-corrected on the pseudofractal). Pipeline
  in figs/mkfigs_com.py, which imports the three papers' own verification scripts
  as backends rather than reimplementing them. Grayscale and regrouped for the 5x8
  trim, as in chapter 7.

  Every number quoted was re-derived from those scripts before being written down:
  the pseudofractal counts to T=7, r_kappa 1095/42 and the exactly-2 slope ratio;
  the diamond RG eigenvalues {2,4}, density -> ln 2, r_kappa 12/44 vs 684; the
  ring's Table I/II and the (c-1)ln2 slopes for c=1..5.

PREFACE IMPLICATIONS (for the rewrite at the end)

  * The book now has exact theory where it had simulation. The preface's line about
    "favoring mean-field arguments that make the mechanisms transparent over
    exhaustive rigor" undersells chapters 6 and 7, which now contain exactly
    solvable results (the ring, the diamond, the (m-n)ln2 law).
  * "Emergence is arithmetic — extensive evidence versus logarithmic cost" is
    arguably the deepest single sentence in the book and belongs in the preface's
    statement of the thesis.
  * Cycles are now the through-line: they multiply shortest paths (ch. 7) AND carve
    communities (ch. 6). The preface currently sells locality as the unifier; short
    cycles are the sharper claim.
  * The chapter now debuts a small new result (the (m-n)ln2 law). If the preface
    claims the book only synthesizes published work, that needs adjusting — or the
    result should be published first.
  * Chapter 6 is now the longest and most technical in the book (~1120 lines, pages
    49-76, twelve sections, nine calculation boxes). It reaches Ising/Potts
    thermodynamics and Migdal-Kadanoff decimation. The preface promises "the core
    ideas are told in plain language, and the more technical calculations are set in
    boxes that can be skipped" — still true clause by clause, but a reader who skips
    every box in 6.6-6.11 will get little from them. Consider warning in the preface
    that the last third of ch. 6 is heavier going, or flagging 6.6-6.11 as optional
    for the general reader.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-07-16 — Chapter 1, "Introduction": real data in "What the data demand"
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The 1st-edition section of this name asserted, on citations alone, that real
networks fall into two families. It now measures four of them and shows the reader.
Networks come from the Netzschleuder repository (networks.skewed.com) through the
graph-tool interface, and are exactly the systems the section already named:

  Internet (AS)            internet_as                  n=22,963   technological
  Gnutella                 gnutella/31                  n=62,561   technological
  Yeast proteins           interactome_yeast            n=1,458    biological
  cond-mat co-authorship   arxiv_collab/cond-mat-2005   n=36,458   social

NEW: Table 1.1 — the small world in two numbers per network. Average distance is
4-7 hops in networks of up to sixty thousand nodes, and every network is far more
cliquish than chance. Two nulls are given, and the difference between them is the
point:
  * <c>_ER, a random network of the same n and <k> — the comparison Watts and
    Strogatz made, and the one the text already invoked. Real networks beat it by
    40x to 2500x.
  * <c>_rew, the same network with links shuffled but every degree held fixed —
    the null the rest of the book insists on. Real networks still beat it, by 2x
    to 333x. So the cliquishness is not merely a shadow of the degree distribution.

NEW: Figure 1.6 — p(k), <c>(k) and <k_nn>(k) for all four. The clustering
hierarchy is visible in all four (measured slopes -0.6 to -1.3, against the
advertised k^-1), and the two families part company on <k_nn>(k) and ONLY there:
falling for Internet/Gnutella/yeast, rising for co-authorship. The assortativity
r says it in one number: -0.052, -0.036, -0.034, and +0.055.

HONESTY NOTES CARRIED IN THE TEXT

  * Gnutella is named as a partial exception: its protocol caps how many peers a
    client holds, so its tail is cut short and it is the least scale-free of the
    four (fitted tail exponent ~5.6, outside the "typically between 2 and 3" the
    section states two pages earlier). The text says so rather than quietly
    dropping the network — it is one the section itself names.
  * The measured <c>(k) slopes (-0.6 to -1.3) bracket but do not equal the
    advertised -1. The text says "of about the advertised steepness". This also
    supports the caution already in section 1.2 (soffer2005) that part of the
    decay is a by-product of degree correlations rather than modular design.

TWO MEASUREMENT BUGS CAUGHT AND FIXED BEFORE THEY REACHED THE PAGE

  1. <c>(k) is undefined for k=1 (a degree-1 node has no neighbour pairs, so
     c = 0/0); graph-tool returns 0. Since degree-1 nodes dominate the small-k
     bins, including them dragged the low-k end to zero and made <c>(k) appear to
     RISE with degree in three of the four networks — the exact opposite of the
     hierarchy the chapter claims. Restricting to k>=2 flips every slope negative.
     Had this gone unnoticed the figure would have contradicted the book.
  2. The log-binned p(k) was averaging counts per distinct degree rather than
     forming a density. In the far tail every degree occurs exactly once, so the
     mean is pinned at 1 and p(k) went FLAT above k~200 instead of falling.
     Dividing by the bin width fixes it; the Internet now falls over four decades.

  A third trap avoided: the textbook configuration-model clustering formula,
  (<k^2>-<k>)^2/(n<k>^3), returns 0.70 for the Internet — ABOVE its measured 0.23,
  and near 1, which is nonsense. The formula assumes <k^2> converges, and at
  gamma ~ 2 it does not. The table therefore reports a MEASURED rewiring (0.125),
  which puts the Internet above its null where the formula put it below. The
  formula is still computed and kept in the CSV as c_conf_formula, for the record.

FIGURE LAYOUT

  The data figure was split in two rather than run to four panels, which also
  fixed a structural problem: section 1.2 had been forward-referencing a figure
  that lived in 1.4.
    Fig. 1.7 (in 1.2, beside the Eq. (1.5) discussion) -- <c>(k) and <c~>(k),
      shared vertical scale.
    Fig. 1.8 (in 1.4, "What the data demand") -- p(k) and <k_nn>(k).
  Each names the other. Legends use short network names; the captions carry the
  full ones, since a half-width panel on a 5x8 page cannot hold "cond-mat
  co-authorship".

PIPELINE

  figs/fetch_intro_data.py measures (needs graph-tool + zstandard + a network
  connection) and writes data/intro_summary.csv and data/intro_curves.csv;
  figs/mkfigs_intro.py plots from those CSVs alone. The split means the figure
  stays reproducible for anyone without graph-tool, which is most people. The
  script's docstring records the brew + venv recipe that makes graph-tool and
  zstandard visible to one interpreter.

NEW (same day, second pass): Eq. (1.5) expanded on Soffer and Vazquez,
Phys. Rev. E 71, 057101 (2005) — "Clustering coefficient without degree
correlations biases".

  The 1st edition disposed of this in one sentence ("when one strips out this bias
  ... it is often roughly constant"). It now gets the several pages it deserves,
  because the paper's claim is that the clustering hierarchy — one of the four
  regularities the book sets out to explain — is LARGELY AN ARTEFACT of the measure.

  * The bias, stated plainly. c_i divides the triangles by k_i(k_i-1)/2, the number
    of neighbour PAIRS, on the grounds that this is how many links the neighbours
    could have. But a neighbour j has only k_j - 1 links left after reaching i, so
    it can touch at most min(k_i,k_j) - 1 of i's other neighbours. The denominator
    counts pairs the degrees forbid.
  * New Figure 1.6, and new Eq. (1.6). A hub of degree k whose k neighbours all have
    degree two, each paired with another neighbour, is AS CLUSTERED AS ITS DEGREES
    ALLOW — no rewiring could add a link among them. Yet c = 1/(k-1) -> 0. The
    standard measure reports a maximally clustered neighbourhood as unclustered.
    In a disassortative network that describes every hub, so the measure and the
    correlations are entangled by construction.
  * New calculation box: omega_i, the largest number of links the neighbours can
    afford, with the bound Omega_i = floor( (1/2) sum_j [min(k_i,k_j) - 1] ) <=
    C(k_i,2) and the Havel-Hakimi greedy that computes omega_i exactly. Then
    c~_i = e_i / omega_i, with its three properties: undefined when omega_i = 0 (a
    hub of leaves has no clustering to speak of, which is the honest verdict);
    c~_i >= c_i always; and c~_i = c_i when every neighbour is at least as well
    connected as the node, so nothing changes where nothing was wrong.
  * OUR OWN DATA REPRODUCE THE PAPER. A dedicated clustering figure (Fig. 1.7)
    puts <c>(k) and <c~>(k) side by side on a SHARED vertical scale, so the
    flattening is read off directly. For the
    Internet the fitted slope moves from -0.76 to +0.02 — the hierarchy vanishes —
    and <c~>(k) varies by a factor of 1.5 across four decades of degree. The paper
    reports "no more than a factor of two" for its (2001-vintage) Internet; we get
    1.5 on a different dataset. Every other slope moves sharply toward zero too.
    Table 1.1 gains a <c~> column: 0.386 (Internet), 0.012 (Gnutella), 0.215
    (yeast), 0.800 (cond-mat). Our cond-mat pair (0.657 -> 0.800) lands almost on
    the paper's co-authorship row (0.65 -> 0.78).
  * The omega_i implementation was verified against the paper's own Fig. 2 worked
    example (k_i=5, neighbour degrees {8,7,2,2,2} -> omega_i=4, Omega_i=5,
    C(k_i,2)=10) before being run on anything.

  WHAT THE TEXT NOW SAYS, AND WHY IT MATTERS TO THE WHOLE BOOK

  Eq. (1.5) is kept, but reframed: it is a robust MEASUREMENT and a weak piece of
  EVIDENCE. A large part of what it records is the degree correlations reflected
  back through a biased denominator, so the hierarchy and the correlations are not
  two independent regularities but two views of one.

  Two facts are stated as surviving the correction, and both matter downstream:
  (1) corrected clustering is still HIGH (0.39 Internet, 0.80 cond-mat), so real
  networks are genuinely cliquish — it is the TREND with degree, not the
  cliquishness, that the bias manufactured; (2) that cliquishness exceeds what the
  degrees AND their correlations can account for, since a degree-preserving
  rewiring lowers it. This is the paper's own point ("the Internet is more clustered
  than expected from the degree distribution and degree correlations alone") and it
  is what leaves the book's thesis standing.

  A pointed closing paragraph was added: when chapters 2-4 derive <c>(k) ~ 1/k from
  a local rule, they are reproducing what the standard measure reports of real
  networks, biases and all — which is the right target — but the deeper quantity
  being matched is the degree correlations plus genuine cliquishness underneath.
  Someone should decide whether those chapters want a sentence of their own on this.

PREFACE IMPLICATIONS (for the rewrite at the end)

  * "The empirical data set the agenda throughout" is now closer to true: chapters
    1, 6 and 7 carry data. Chapters 2-5 still have none. If they stay that way, the
    preface should say the data appear at the start and at the end rather than
    throughout.
  * The Introduction now opens the book's empirical case rather than deferring it,
    which strengthens the "held up against real data" promise in the plan-of-the-
    book section.
  * The Introduction now undercuts one of the four regularities it advertises, on
    p. 8, using the author's own paper. This is a strength, but the preface's list
    of "what recurs across systems" should probably name the clustering hierarchy
    more carefully — or the preface can make a virtue of it: the book takes its own
    measures apart before explaining them.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-07-16 — Chapter 8, "Outlook": section 8.2 updated; proofreading edits
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 8.2, "What remains to be done", rewritten against what chapters 6 and 7
now contain. Two of its three concrete open problems had been overtaken by the new
material and were misleading as written. Structure (concrete / programmatic /
widening) and the closing line are unchanged.

  * The exponential law is still open, but no longer unbounded: the diamond gives
    an exact ceiling (stretched exponential in sqrt(n)) that every stochastic rule
    falls below. The question is now "where between the log-quadratic floor and the
    deterministic ceiling does a rule land, and why does copying climb so far?"
  * The Ramsey number's method-dependence said only that it "remains tied" to the
    detection method. That is far too gentle now: it is a factor of 25 on the same
    web (1095 plain vs 42 degree-corrected), and the two rules disagree about which
    cut wins. But the same calculations supply the repair the old text merely wished
    for — the evidence density -> ln K per link is method- and lattice-independent.
    The item now names the invariant a better definition could be built on.
  * NEW: the mean field knows only c_2, yet real networks fall either side of the
    c_2/e line by the sign of their correlations. The clearest gap between the
    calculation and the data in chapter 7.
  * NEW, and spanning both chapters: the ring is split exactly when links outnumber
    nodes — when independent cycles become extensive — and cycles are also what
    multiply shortest paths. If that accounting is general rather than an accident
    of the ring, the last two chapters fold into one statement.
  * NEW, two clue-like loose ends: sqrt(n) for the number of communities is
    numerical, not proved; and the even-odd alternation in r_kappa vs cycle length
    is "too regular to be noise and has no explanation".
  * NEW programmatic lead: every closed form in the last two chapters lives on a
    DETERMINISTIC lattice with the partition handed in. That is the price of exact
    solvability — the results bound the simulated ones without reproducing them.
    Whether the RG map survives off-lattice is the frontier, and it is hard
    precisely because detectability there is itself a phase transition.

PROOFREADING EDITS (chapter 6)

  * Dropped "because the paper does not" from the ring symmetry-breaking passage —
    no need to score a point off the source.
  * Eq. (6.32), the closed form for r_kappa(b,s;q), stacked vertically with
    `aligned` instead of running the two formulas side by side. Single equation
    number retained, so nothing downstream renumbers.
  * The pseudofractal web is now credited to Dorogovtsev, Goltsev and Mendes (2002)
    at the point where section 6.9 defines it. The entry was already in
    references.bib and already cited in chapter 7; only the model's own definition
    lacked it.

FIGURE FIX (chapter 7)

  Fig. 7.6's caption was being pushed off the bottom of the page. The cause was not
  the panel spacing: the figure's natural width was 0.78W = 3.13in while the chapter
  includes it at width=\linewidth = 4.05in, so LaTeX scaled it up 29% and its 5.14in
  height became 6.64in — essentially the whole 6.8in text block. Rebuilt at full
  natural width (so width=\linewidth barely scales it) with hspace 0.55 -> 0.45; it
  now occupies 4.31in and the caption fits with room to spare. A comment in
  figs/mkfigs.py records the trap, since "make the figure smaller" makes it worse.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTE FOR THE AUTHOR (not a book change)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

arXiv:2605.25237, Conclusions, contains a typo worth fixing upstream: it says the
multiplicity is "below the MF approximation" for BOTH assortative and disassortative
networks, with "In contrast" between the two sentences. The data say the opposite for
assortative — cond-mat sits above the mean field in 100% of its snapshots, the Internet
below in 100% of its. The book states it correctly.

arXiv:2607.01967 (the ring), two items:

 1. The stated reason for the c=1 result is wrong. The manuscript says "A plain cycle
    offers no density contrast to exploit." The within-arc/whole-ring density contrast
    is ~2 for every c, cycle included (1.998, 1.996, 1.994 for c=1,2,3 at n=1024). The
    contrast is identical; only the number of edges enjoying it changes.

 2. The general law log R = (m - n) ln 2 = (c-1) n ln 2 answers the paper's own open
    question ("the scaling of r_kappa with larger c") and is worth adding to the paper.
    It follows from gain (ln 2 per edge) minus cost (log C(n,n/2) ~ n ln 2 for the
    labels), the label term being exactly -log C(n,n/2) at alpha=1. Verified for
    c=1..5. It reframes the paper's headline: c=1 is not a threshold but a knife-edge
    cancellation at the zero of a smooth family, and it connects the ring to the
    diamond paper's log R_dc/m -> ln K (the ring's ln 2 IS ln K for K=2).

 Also worth settling in the paper, both cheap: the balanced cut is confirmed optimal
 over all n_1 (the profile rises monotonically to n/2), and r_kappa saturates at ~20
 for large c rather than shrinking indefinitely.

